ITEMS = [
("A new FortiGate is powered on with factory settings. What is the default administrator username?",
 ["admin", "root", "fortinet", "maintainer"], "A",
 "The factory default account is admin with no password, and FortiOS makes you set a new password at the first login. root and fortinet are not FortiGate login names."),
("An administrator wants an admin account usable only from the 10.1.1.0/24 management subnet. What should be configured?",
 ["A firewall policy to port1", "Trusted hosts on the account", "A read-only admin profile", "A virtual IP for HTTPS access"], "B",
 "Trusted hosts on an administrator account limit the source addresses that account can log in from. Firewall policies control traffic through the FortiGate, not administrative traffic to it."),
("Which interface setting controls whether administrators can reach the GUI through that interface?",
 ["Interface role", "Device detection", "Administrative access", "Estimated bandwidth fields"], "C",
 "Administrative access (allowaccess) lists the management protocols, such as HTTPS, SSH and PING, the interface answers. The role only changes which options the GUI shows."),
("By default, how many failed login attempts lock out an administrator's source IP?",
 ["One", "Five", "Ten", "Three"], "D",
 "The admin-lockout-threshold default is 3 failed attempts, after which the source IP is locked out for admin-lockout-duration, 60 seconds by default."),
("Which admin profile grants full access to every setting, including global settings and all VDOMs?",
 ["super_admin", "prof_admin", "root_admin", "read_only"], "A",
 "super_admin is the built-in profile with full read-write access everywhere. prof_admin is the built-in profile for administrators limited to their own VDOM."),
("Without changes, how long can an idle GUI administrator session last before logout?",
 ["1 minute", "5 minutes", "15 minutes", "30 minutes"], "B",
 "The global admintimeout default is 5 minutes of idle time. It can be raised in System Settings, but a short timeout is the safer default."),
("How many FortiToken Mobile licenses come free with a FortiGate?",
 ["None", "One", "Two", "Ten"], "C",
 "Every FortiGate includes two free FortiToken Mobile licenses, enough to test two-factor authentication or protect two administrator accounts. More are bought as license packs."),
("An administrator restores a configuration backup from the GUI. What happens next?",
 ["Changes apply with no restart", "The firmware is reinstalled", "Only changed settings load", "The FortiGate reboots"], "D",
 "Restoring a full configuration file replaces the running configuration and reboots the FortiGate. Plan it for a maintenance window."),
("A configuration backup was saved with encryption enabled. What is needed to restore it?",
 ["The password set at backup", "The unit's FortiCare account", "The admin's FortiToken code", "A valid FortiGuard license"], "A",
 "An encrypted backup can only be restored by entering the password chosen when it was saved. Losing that password makes the file unusable."),
("Before upgrading FortiOS across several major releases, what should the administrator follow?",
 ["The latest image only", "The documented upgrade path", "The oldest image first", "The FortiAnalyzer release notes"], "B",
 "Fortinet publishes an upgrade path that lists the intermediate builds to install in order. Jumping straight to the latest image can lose or corrupt configuration settings."),
("Which FortiGuard service is queried in real time rather than downloaded as a package?",
 ["Antivirus signatures", "IPS signatures", "Web filter ratings", "Application signatures"], "C",
 "FortiGate asks FortiGuard for a URL's category when it sees the URL, caching the answer. Antivirus, IPS and application control signatures are downloaded as packages."),
("Which CLI command shows the firmware version, serial number and operation mode?",
 ["diagnose sys top", "diagnose autoupdate versions", "get system performance status", "get system status"], "D",
 "get system status prints the version, serial number, operation mode and VDOM settings. diagnose autoupdate versions lists the FortiGuard database versions."),
("In multi-VDOM mode, which VDOM is the management VDOM by default?",
 ["root", "global", "mgmt", "default"], "A",
 "The root VDOM exists on every FortiGate and is the management VDOM by default, so FortiGuard, logging and NTP traffic leave from it. global is a configuration scope, not a VDOM."),
("Two VDOMs on the same FortiGate must pass traffic to each other without leaving the device. What connects them?",
 ["A VLAN trunk", "An inter-VDOM link", "A loopback interface", "A software switch"], "B",
 "An inter-VDOM link is a pair of virtual interfaces, one in each VDOM, that routes traffic between them inside the FortiGate. Policies on each side still control what passes."),
("Which settings are configured in the global scope rather than inside a VDOM?",
 ["Firewall policies", "Address objects", "HA and firmware", "Static routes"], "C",
 "High availability, firmware, administrators and FortiGuard settings are global. Policies, objects and routes belong to each VDOM."),
("A VDOM runs in transparent mode. How does it forward traffic between its interfaces?",
 ["By routing table lookup", "By policy routes only", "By SD-WAN rules", "By MAC address table"], "D",
 "A transparent mode VDOM works like a bridge, forwarding frames by MAC address, while firewall policies still inspect the traffic. Its interfaces carry no IP addresses."),
("What must a transparent mode VDOM have so administrators can reach it?",
 ["A management IP address", "An IP on every interface", "A default virtual IP", "A dedicated HA link"], "A",
 "Interfaces in transparent mode have no IP addresses, so the VDOM gets one management IP for administrative access and for FortiGuard updates."),
("One port must carry three tagged networks to a switch. What should the administrator create on that port?",
 ["Three hardware switches", "Three VLAN interfaces", "Three loopback interfaces", "Three interface zones"], "B",
 "VLAN interfaces are 802.1Q subinterfaces of a physical port, one per tag, each with its own IP and policies. Zones only group interfaces for policy."),
("Which interface type bundles links with LACP so every member carries traffic?",
 ["Redundant interface", "Software switch", "802.3ad aggregate", "Hardware switch"], "C",
 "An 802.3ad aggregate uses LACP and spreads traffic over all its members. A redundant interface keeps only one member active at a time."),
("An administrator sets port1's addressing mode to DHCP. What does the FortiGate do?",
 ["Hands out leases on port1", "Relays DHCP requests to a server", "Uses a link-local address", "Gets its own address on port1"], "D",
 "The addressing mode is how the interface itself gets an address, so DHCP makes port1 a DHCP client. Serving leases is a separate DHCP server setting on the interface."),
("Which admin profile permission lets an administrator view a section but not change it?",
 ["Read", "Read-Write", "None", "Custom"], "A",
 "Each area of an admin profile is set to None, Read or Read-Write. Read shows the pages without letting the administrator save changes."),
("A script authenticates to the FortiGate REST API. What kind of administrator should it use?",
 ["A super_admin with password", "A REST API admin with a key", "The default admin account", "A local user with a token"], "B",
 "A REST API administrator authenticates with a generated API key and should be limited by trusted hosts and a narrow profile. Sharing an interactive admin's password with scripts is poor practice."),
("Which command returns a FortiGate to factory default settings?",
 ["execute reboot", "execute restore config", "execute factoryreset", "execute shutdown"], "C",
 "execute factoryreset erases the configuration and restarts the unit with defaults. execute restore config loads a saved configuration instead."),
("Which contract entitles a FortiGate to firmware updates and technical support?",
 ["FortiGuard IPS service", "FortiToken Mobile license", "An extra VDOM license", "FortiCare support"], "D",
 "FortiCare is the support contract that covers firmware access, hardware replacement and technical support. FortiGuard subscriptions cover security services such as IPS and web filtering."),
]
assert len(ITEMS) == 24
for i, it in enumerate(ITEMS):
    assert it[2] == "ABCD"[i % 4], i
